<body>
  <GameNav />
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Rooms &amp; Exits</a> &gt; <a href="/rooms-and-exits/">Chapter 2</a> &gt; Level 11</nav>

  <main class="seo-section">
    <h1>Rooms &amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 11 Walkthrough: DIY Shop</h1>

    <div class="disclaimer">Unofficial fan-made guide. Not affiliated with Webelinx Games, the developer of Rooms &amp; Exits: Escape Room Games.</div>

    <div class="verify-note">Rebuilt frame by frame from a recorded playthrough of the current build — 5 minutes 56 seconds of 1280×720 footage, sampled at one frame per second across the whole run and at four frames per second over every lock. The five-dial combination lock (42130), the letter lock (GDC) and the keypad number (954) were each read on at least two separate frames and the readings agree. The sticky note (51432) was read twice from two different frames. Where the footage cannot settle a rule — the wiring behind the five-button panel, and what the five neon figures are supposed to mean — this page says so instead of inventing an explanation.</div>

    <LevelSelector chapter={2} current={11} variant="jump" label="Chapter 2 · No Honor Among Thieves" />

    <div class="answer-box">
      <span class="label">Quick answer</span>
      <p>The DIY Shop runs on four numbers and one word: <b>51432</b> on the sticky note, <b>42130</b> on the five-dial combination lock, <b>GDC</b> on the letter lock and <b>954</b> on the tracing paper. Finish worth <b>+50 coins</b>.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Read the yellow <b>sticky note</b> clipped to the trolley → <b>51432</b>.</li>
        <li>Press the five <b>yellow buttons</b> on the blue panel in that order (counted 1–5 from the left: 5, 1, 4, 3, 2) → the tool chest drawer opens → <b>Hammer</b>.</li>
        <li>Hammer the <b>brick wall</b> → a recess with a <b>five-dial combination lock</b>.</li>
        <li>Use the <b>UV lamp</b> on the blue neon board, then set the dials to <b>42130</b> → the green cabinet opens → <b>Blowtorch part</b>.</li>
        <li><b>Combine</b> the Blowtorch part with the other two blowtorch pieces → <b>Blowtorch</b>, then with the <b>Lighter</b> → <b>Lit blowtorch</b>. <b>Dismantle</b> the <b>Hanger</b> → <b>Hook</b>.</li>
        <li>Burn the <b>floor grate</b>, hook out the <b>Key</b>, open the blue tool chest drawer → <b>Blueprint</b>.</li>
        <li>Use the blueprint on the <b>drawing board</b> → its lettered rooms include <b>G, D, C</b>. Set the green toolbox lock to <b>GDC</b> → <b>Crowbar</b>.</li>
        <li>Pry the loose <b>floorboards</b> → tracing paper <b>954</b>. Enter it on the wall <b>keypad</b> → three red <b>valves</b>; turn all three → <b>+50 coins</b>.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="video-wrap">
            <div class="yt-facade" data-yt="b9P7MK7_zDs" data-title="Rooms &amp;amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 11 DIY Shop walkthrough"
           role="button" tabindex="0" aria-label="Play video: Rooms &amp;amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 11 DIY Shop walkthrough">
        <img src="https://i.ytimg.com/vi/b9P7MK7_zDs/maxresdefault.jpg"
             alt="Rooms &amp;amp; Exits Chapter 2 Level 11 DIY Shop walkthrough" loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1280" height="720"
             onerror="this.onerror=null;this.src='https://i.ytimg.com/vi/b9P7MK7_zDs/hqdefault.jpg'">
      </div>
    </div>

    <img class="hero" src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l11-hero-diy-shop.jpg" alt="Rooms and Exits Chapter 2 Level 11 DIY Shop: the whole workshop in isometric view, with stud walls, a workbench and miter saw, red cement bags on a pallet, a brick wall on the right and the exit door" width="1200" height="594" />

    <h2>What this level actually is<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The DIY Shop is the eleventh room of Chapter 2 and it is not a shop you can shop in — it is a building site with a shop's name. One half-finished workshop, seen in a single isometric shot: a wall framed in bare studs on the left with a chain hanging off it, a stretch of oriented strand board with the electrics exposed, a back wall with wooden stairs going up to a platform, and a finished cinder-block wall on the right with a white service door, a blue workbench and a security camera above it. In the middle of the floor sits a mitre saw on a wooden bench, a pallet of red cement bags, a stack of grey blocks, and two hand trucks loaded with cardboard boxes. There is a square metal grate in the concrete near the bench and a brick wall standing on its own at the back right, which is the strangest thing in the room until you hit it with a hammer.</p>
    <p>That is the whole level in one glance, and it is also why people stall in it. Nothing about the DIY Shop announces a sequence. There are four locks in this room and around them a dozen objects that look like they might be clues: a kettle, paint cans, a coil of cable, a hard hat, a spirit level, a toolbox with a handle and no lock on it. The game will let you tap all of them. None of them are the answer. What actually moves you forward is a sticky note clipped to a trolley, a UV lamp that only matters on one blue board, a key at the bottom of a drain, and a plan on a drawing board that spells three letters.</p>
    <p>The other thing worth saying up front is that the DIY Shop is a two-area level pretending to be a one-area level. The workshop is where you start, and it holds the first three locks. The back room — the one with the pink insulation panels, the paint cans and the pallets — is where the last two locks live, and you do not get there until the blueprint has been read. Players who spend ten minutes circling the workshop looking for the letter lock are looking in the wrong room. The recording spends roughly three and a half minutes in the workshop and two in the back room, and the split is almost exactly at the drawing board.</p>
    <p>Finally, the theme is not decoration. The room has a gas leak — that is the thing you are here to fix, and it is the reason the level ends where it does, at three red valves behind a keypad panel rather than at a door. Every lock in the DIY Shop is on the way to those valves, and the last one you open is literally the gas line. Once you know that, the order of the room stops feeling arbitrary: you are working from the outside of the building inwards, and the valves are the centre.</p>

    <h2>Step by step<span class="accent">.</span></h2>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 1 · 0:04</span>
      <strong>Read the room before you touch anything</strong>
      The opening sweep is the only wide shot you get without an inventory panel over half the screen, so use it. Left: the stud wall with the chain, the OSB panels and the open cutout with the wiring. Back: the wooden stairs, the platform with the blue tarp, and the door at the top. Right: the cinder-block wall, the white service door, the blue workbench, the camera. Middle: the mitre saw bench, the cement pallet, the block stack, the hand trucks and, on the floor, the square metal grate. Back right: the free-standing brick wall. That brick wall is the single most important object in the shot and it looks like scenery. Remember where it is.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b9P7MK7_zDs&amp;t=4s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:04</a></div>
    </div>
    <InContentAd />

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 2 · 0:44</span>
      <strong>The sticky note on the trolley: 51432</strong>
      There is a metal trolley against the blue wall with translucent yellow trays on it — a pen, a spiral notebook, a couple of tools. Clipped to the front rail, between the top and middle trays, is a yellow sticky note with five handwritten digits on it: <b>51432</b>. This is the first code in the level and the only one that is written down in plain sight. It is also the one most people walk past, because the note is small, the trays are bright yellow and the eye goes to the trays.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l11-sticky-note-51432.jpg" alt="The yellow sticky note reading 51432 clipped to the metal trolley in the DIY Shop" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b9P7MK7_zDs&amp;t=44s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:44</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 3 · 0:57</span>
      <strong>Open the bag and meet the Lighter</strong>
      The backpack icon at the top right opens a grid of slots with four verbs under it: <b>Use</b>, <b>Combine</b>, <b>Dismantle</b>, <b>Examine</b>. At this point the bag holds a <b>Lighter</b> — a red disposable lighter with a silver top — and the recording opens it, selects the lighter and examines it in a full-screen close-up. It is worth doing the same, not because the lighter is a puzzle, but because it is the ignition for the only flame in the level: much later you will press <b>Combine</b> on the assembled blowtorch and tap this lighter, and the game will not offer that option until both items are in the bag. A lighter on its own does nothing here.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b9P7MK7_zDs&amp;t=57s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">0:57</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 4 · 1:30</span>
      <strong>The blue panel with five yellow buttons</strong>
      On the blue wall, above the blue tool chest with the hard hat on it, there is a rectangular panel: a textured blue face, a row of five small indicator lights across the top, a grey strip with a triangular play symbol in the middle, and five round yellow buttons along the bottom. The buttons are identical and unlabelled. There is no number on any of them, which is exactly the trick: the note gives the order and the panel gives you nothing but five identical targets.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l11-button-panel.jpg" alt="The blue panel with five round yellow buttons, five indicator lights and a play button in the DIY Shop" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b9P7MK7_zDs&amp;t=90s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:30</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 5 · 1:40</span>
      <strong>Press 5, 1, 4, 3, 2 and watch the lights</strong>
      Count the five buttons 1 to 5 from the left and press them in the order written on the sticky note: <b>fifth, first, fourth, third, second</b> — that is 5, 1, 4, 3, 2. Each correct press turns one of the indicator lights green; a wrong press resets the row. You are not being timed, so press slowly and check the lights between presses. When all five are green the panel is done and the top drawer of the blue tool chest below it slides open. This is the one place in the level where the code and the lock are ten feet apart and both are on screen at the same time — if you are stuck on the panel, walk back to the trolley and read the note again.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b9P7MK7_zDs&amp;t=100s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:40</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 6 · 1:42</span>
      <strong>Take the Hammer out of the tool chest</strong>
      The open drawer holds a single claw hammer with a wooden handle and a steel head. Tap it and it goes into the bag with the label <b>Hammer</b>. There is nothing to combine it with and nowhere to equip it — in this game you select it from the bag and press <b>Use</b>, then tap the thing you want to hit. The game does not tell you what to hit. It does not have to: there is exactly one brick wall in the DIY Shop and exactly one object in the room that a claw hammer obviously belongs against.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b9P7MK7_zDs&amp;t=102s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:42</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 7 · 1:50</span>
      <strong>Hammer the brick wall, find the combination lock</strong>
      Select the Hammer, press <b>Use</b> and tap the free-standing brick wall. The bricks break away in a jagged hole and behind them is a shallow recess with a green double door and a silver five-dial combination lock mounted in front of it. This is the moment the level changes shape: you came in with one clue on a sticky note and now you have a lock with five wheels, no visible code, and a blue neon board on the other side of the room that you have probably already tapped once and found dark.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l11-brick-wall.jpg" alt="The broken brick wall with the hammer still against it, revealing a recess and the five-dial combination lock" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b9P7MK7_zDs&amp;t=110s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:50</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 8 · 1:56</span>
      <strong>The UV lamp and the blue neon board</strong>
      The blue board on the wall is a row of neon tubes bent into five separate figures, and in ordinary light it is just a decoration. Select the <b>UV lamp</b> from the bag, press <b>Use</b> and tap the board: it opens into a full-screen close-up and the tubes light up, each figure built from a magenta spine with cyan limbs. Our capture shows the five shapes clearly — a trident-like fork, a hanging sign, an arrow, a barred vertical, and a framed rectangle — and they do not look like printed digits. The game is using them as the code for the five-dial combination lock; how the five shapes map onto 42130 is the part of this level we could not reconstruct from the footage, and the box further down this page says so plainly.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l11-uv-neon-board.jpg" alt="The blue neon board lit up by the UV lamp, showing five glowing tube figures in cyan and magenta" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b9P7MK7_zDs&amp;t=116s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">1:56</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 9 · 2:30</span>
      <strong>Set the five dials to 42130</strong>
      Back at the combination lock. Each of the five wheels shows a column of numbers with one at the pointer, and the wheels turn in both directions. Set them left to right to <b>4, 2, 1, 3, 0</b> and the lock releases. The green double doors behind the bricks swing open on an empty-looking interior, and the only thing inside is a yellow canister with a nozzle: the <b>Blowtorch part</b>. Take it. If the lock will not take the code, check that you are reading the middle row at the pointer rather than the row above or below it — the wheels show three numbers at once and that is the most common way to mis-set one.
      <img src="/rooms-and-exits/c2l11-dial-lock-42130.jpg" alt="The five-dial combination lock with 42130 lined up at the pointer, inside the recess behind the brick wall" width="1200" height="594" loading="lazy" />
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b9P7MK7_zDs&amp;t=150s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:30</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 10 · 2:44</span>
      <strong>Build the Blowtorch in the bag</strong>
      Open the bag. By now it holds three separate blowtorch components — the yellow body you just took, a slim metal nozzle and a red fuel canister, the last two already in the bag from earlier in the level. Select the <b>Blowtorch part</b>, press <b>Combine</b> and tap the other two in turn: the three slots resolve into one item labelled <b>Blowtorch</b>. It is not lit yet and it will not work on anything until it is. Players who skip this step carry a useless yellow canister to the drain and conclude the grate is not interactive.
      <div class="ts">Watch: <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=b9P7MK7_zDs&amp;t=164s" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2:44</a></div>
    </div>

    <div class="step-card">
      <span class="step-num">Step 11 · 3:04</span>
      <strong>Collect the Hanger</strong>
      On the workshop side, near the mitre-saw bench under the window, there is a wire coat hanger — the ordinary kind, thin wire with a hook at the top. Get close enough and the game outlines it and labels it <b>Hanger</b>; tap it and it goes into the bag. It looks like junk and it is not: it is the only tool in the level that can reach down a drain, and the way you turn it into that tool is not obvious.
