    <h2>The four codes, and what each one opens<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>There are four codes in the DIY Shop and they are not interchangeable. Every one of them belongs to exactly one lock, and the locks are not in the order you find the codes. This table is the shortest version of the level:</p>
    <table class="code-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Code</th><th>Where it is written</th><th>What it opens</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><b>51432</b></td><td>Yellow sticky note clipped to the trolley in the workshop</td><td>The blue five-button panel → tool chest drawer → <b>Hammer</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>42130</b></td><td>Five neon figures on the blue board, read with the UV lamp</td><td>The five-dial combination lock behind the brick wall → green cabinet → <b>Blowtorch part</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>GDC</b></td><td>The blueprint on the drawing board — its lettered rooms include G, D and C</td><td>The three-letter lock on the green toolbox → <b>Crowbar</b></td></tr>
        <tr><td><b>954</b></td><td>Tracing paper under the loose floorboards</td><td>The wall keypad → gas valve panel → end of level</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>Two of these are digits and two are not, and the game does you no favours by mixing them in one room. The five-button panel and the five-dial combination lock both want five characters, which is the single most common source of confusion in this level: players assume the sticky note's 51432 must belong to the combination lock because both are five digits long, spend ten minutes trying to make 51432 fit five wheels, and never press the yellow buttons at all. The note and the buttons are ten feet apart on the same wall. The combination lock is behind a brick wall that has to be broken first. Match the clue to the object in front of you, not to the number of characters.</p>
    <p>The letter code has the opposite problem. GDC is short, it is written on a plan rather than a lock, and the plan is a piece of paper you unroll on a board — it reads like background art. If you have a three-letter lock with no letters, the blueprint is the only place in the level where letters exist. And 954 is the only code that is not visible on a surface at eye level; it is under the floorboards, behind a crowbar, behind the letter lock, behind the blueprint. It is the deepest link in the chain and it opens the shallowest lock: three digits, straight into a keypad.</p>

    <h2>Why the order matters<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>The DIY Shop is a dependency chain, not a checklist, and almost every "I'm stuck" in this room is a link that has not been made yet rather than a puzzle that has not been solved. Read it as five gates:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><b>Note → panel.</b> The five-button panel cannot be brute-forced in any reasonable time and it does not respond to the play button alone. It needs 51432, and 51432 only exists on the trolley.</li>
      <li><b>Panel → hammer → wall.</b> The hammer is not hidden. It is inside a drawer that the panel opens. Without the panel you have no hammer, and without the hammer the brick wall is a wall.</li>
      <li><b>UV lamp → board → combination lock.</b> The blue board is inert until the lamp is used on it, and the combination lock cannot be set until the board has been read. Both the lamp and the board are in the workshop, which is why this is the one part of the level that flows cleanly.</li>
      <li><b>Key → blueprint → letter lock.</b> The brass key from the drain opens the tool chest drawer, the drawer holds the blueprint, the blueprint is the letter code. Break that chain anywhere and the green toolbox stays shut.</li>
      <li><b>Tracing paper → keypad → valves.</b> The crowbar from the toolbox lifts the floorboards, the floorboards hide 954, 954 opens the panel, the panel holds the valves. This is the longest chain and the one that sends people back and forth between the two rooms.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Looked at that way the level has exactly one puzzle with a real "aha" in it — the note that is not for the lock it appears to be for — and four dependency checks behind it. The rest is walking. The recording finishes the room in 5:56 of footage and 5:21 on the game clock, and most of that difference is the two trips between the workshop and the back room, not thinking time.</p>

    <div class="warn">
      <b>What we could not verify:</b> the rule behind the five neon figures, and how the blueprint singles out its three letters. The capture shows the blue board lighting up under the UV lamp and it shows the combination lock being set to 42130, and we read that final dial position twice from two separate frames, but the footage never shows a wrong setting being rejected and never shows the board and the lock in the same shot. So we publish the code as the answer and not as a derivation: we cannot tell you how each bent tube is supposed to become a digit, and we are not going to invent a rule for it. The same caution applies to the blueprint — the plan is covered in letter and number labels and G, D and C are among them, but which marking is meant to select those three is not something the footage settles. It also applies to the five-button panel: the note reads 51432 twice over two frames and the lights go green right after, but the wiring between the note and the buttons is our reading of the sequence, not something the game spells out. And the three valves: we can see all three turned and we can read their final angles, but the recording never shows a wrong valve position, so treat left-up-down as the end state rather than as a combination.
    </div>

    <h2>Where people get stuck<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <ul>
      <li><b>The sticky note is not for the combination lock.</b> This is the big one. 51432 and 42130 are both five characters and the combination lock is the more interesting lock, so the note gets carried across the room and forced into five wheels. It belongs to the five yellow buttons on the blue panel, counted 1–5 from the left.</li>
      <li><b>The five yellow buttons have no labels.</b> There is nothing on the panel to tell you which button is "1". Count from the left and commit to that convention. The panel only cares about the order, not about which button you call first.</li>
      <li><b>The brick wall looks like scenery.</b> It stands free of the other walls, it has no highlight, and the camera never lingers on it. It is the only breakable object in the level and the hammer exists for it. If you have the hammer and no combination lock, you have not hit the wall.</li>
      <li><b>The blue board does nothing without the lamp.</b> Tapping it in normal light gives you a row of dark tubes. Players decide it is decoration and never come back with the UV lamp, which is in the bag from the first minute.</li>
      <li><b>The hanger is not the hook.</b> The item you need for the drain is called <b>Hook</b>, and the only way to get it is <b>Dismantle</b> on the Hanger. Use on the drain does nothing until the hanger has been dismantled.</li>
      <li><b>The drain needs two passes.</b> First the lit blowtorch to burn the grate, then the hook to fish the key out. Doing one and walking away is the most common half-finished step in the room.</li>
      <li><b>The blueprint must go on the board.</b> Reading it from the bag shows a rolled tube. The letters only exist once it is unrolled on the drawing board, and the board is blank until then, so it looks like an unfinished prop.</li>
      <li><b>The toolbox is in the other room.</b> The green toolbox is not in the workshop. If you have the letter code and cannot find a letter lock, you are in the wrong half of the level.</li>
      <li><b>The floorboards are loose, not locked.</b> They do not highlight and they do not look different from the fixed boards. The crowbar is the hint, and the tracing paper is under them.</li>
      <li><b>There is no exit door to use.</b> The white service door and the door at the top of the stairs are both scenery. The level ends at the valves, the moment the third one is turned.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What the room is made of<span class="accent">.</span></h2>
    <p>It helps to know which objects are real and which are set dressing, because the DIY Shop is unusually dense with props. Real, in the workshop: the trolley with the sticky note, the blue button panel, the blue tool chest and its two drawers, the brick wall, the blue neon board, the green cabinet behind the bricks, the floor grate, the hanger, the drawing board, and the mitre-saw bench as a landmark. Real, in the back room: the green toolbox, the loose floorboards, the grey keypad panel and the valves behind it. Everything else is texture — the cement bags, the grey blocks, the paint cans and brushes, the scaffolding, the hard hat, the kettle, the cable coil, the security camera, the stairs, the white service door, and the cardboard boxes on the hand trucks.</p>
    <p>That distinction matters for one practical reason: the game's highlight system is inconsistent in this level. Some interactive objects glow when you get close and some do not, and the brick wall, the floorboards and the drawing board are all in the second group. If you are hunting for the next thing to tap, work from the list above rather than from what looks lit. The two objects players most often miss — the wall and the boards — are both things you have to decide to hit rather than things the game offers you.</p>
    <p>The layout also explains the two-room confusion. The workshop and the back room share a wall and the camera makes them look like one continuous space, but they are separate scenes with separate prop sets and separate locks. The workshop owns the first three codes (note, board, combination lock). The back room owns the last two (toolbox, floorboards) plus the ending. Once you have the blueprint, there is no reason to return to the workshop at all — and every minute spent there after that point is a minute spent looking at props.</p>

